course: everyday
lesson: 49
title: Lost outdoors
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: If you are lost in a forest or a park, the most important rule is to stay where you are: a person who keeps still is found much faster than one who keeps moving.
explain: Searchers start where you were last seen and work outwards; if you wander, you may walk out of the area they search, or walk away from them as they come closer.
explain: Children learn to hug a tree: pick a tree, stay right by it and even put your arms round it, because it calms you and keeps you in one place.
explain: Make yourself easy to find: blow three blasts on a whistle, wear or wave something bright, and answer when people call your name; searchers are friends, you are not in trouble, so never hide from them.
explain: A girl lost in a Washington forest as it gets dark keeps warm by sitting on her backpack or on branches instead of the cold ground, zipping up her jacket and staying put until the searchers' lights reach her.
explain: A boy lost in woods in Washington kept walking to find the way out; he went further from the trail, and the search took a day longer than it would have.
explain: A girl lost on a moor in England stayed by a big rock, put on her spare jumper and blew her whistle three times every few minutes; rescuers heard it and found her quickly.
fact: If you are lost outdoors, you stay in one place.
fact: A person who stays still is easier to find than a person who keeps moving.
fact: Searchers begin where you were last seen and search outwards.
fact: Hugging a tree helps a lost child stay calm and stay in one place.
fact: Three blasts on a whistle means you need help.
fact: Bright colours make you easier for searchers to see.
fact: You answer when searchers call your name and never hide from them.
fact: Sitting on branches or a bag keeps you off the cold ground, which takes heat away from your body, and keeps you warmer.
fact: A lost child is never in trouble for being lost.
fact: Before a trip, a family can agree on a meeting place in case someone gets separated.
fact: If you are lost in a town or a shop, you ask a shop worker, a police officer, a worker in uniform such as a lifeguard, or a parent with children for help.
fact: If you are lost and get wet, you find shelter and put on dry or extra layers.
fact: Getting wet and cold when lost outdoors can lead to hypothermia, when the body gets dangerously cold.
exercise: What is the most important thing to do if you are lost in a forest? | Stay where you are | Keep walking until you find a road | Climb the highest hill to look around | Run in the direction you think is home
exercise: Why is it easier to find a child who stays still? | Searchers can reach the place, as the child is not moving away | Still children make more noise | Searchers only look in open clearings, where they can see a long way | Moving children are harder to hear
exercise: Where do searchers start looking for a lost child? | Where the child was last seen | At the child's home | At the nearest town | At the top of the nearest mountain
exercise: Why are lost children taught to hug a tree? | It calms them and keeps them in one place | Trees are always right next to the trail | Trees keep wild animals away | Trees show which way is north
exercise: Which helps searchers see a lost child? | Wearing or waving something brightly coloured | Hiding under thick bushes | Wearing dark green clothes so they blend in with the forest | Lying flat behind a log
exercise: How can a lost child call for help without shouting until her voice goes? | Blow three blasts on a whistle | Bang two stones softly | Sing quietly to herself | Whistle with her lips once an hour
exercise: A lost boy hears strangers calling his name. What should he do? | Answer them loudly, because they are searchers | Hide, because they are strangers and he must never talk to them | Stay quiet in case he is in trouble | Run away from the voices
exercise: Why should a lost child sit on branches or a backpack instead of the ground? | The cold ground takes heat away from the body | Ants will not bite on a backpack | It keeps clothes looking clean | Searchers only look for children who are sitting up high
exercise: Will a lost child be in trouble when found? | No, everyone will just be glad to find them | Yes, they will be punished for getting lost | Only if they were lost for a long time | Only if the police had to come
exercise: What can a family agree on before a big day out to help if someone gets separated? | A meeting place to go to | A secret word for strangers | Which child walks first | What time to have lunch
exercise: A girl is separated from her dad in a busy shopping centre. Whom should she ask for help? | A shop worker at a counter or a parent with children | The first teenager she sees | Nobody, she should go and search the car park on her own | A person sitting alone in a car
exercise: Why should a lost child not try to find the way out alone as it gets dark? | She may wander further from the searchers and get hurt | The forest locks all its gates at night and she would be shut in | Walking makes her more hungry | The trail moves in the dark
exercise: What should a lost child do when it starts to get dark? | Stay put, keep warm and make noise when she hears people | Walk faster and faster so she gets out of the forest before dark | Lie down on the cold ground to rest | Hide in a deep hole to stay safe
test: A boy in Oregon loses sight of his family on a forest trail and cannot see the path. What should he do first? | Stop, stay by a tree and blow his whistle | Walk on to look for the car park | Go back the way he thinks he came, off the trail | Climb a tall tree to look for his family
test: Two searchers walk towards a lost girl while she walks away to look for them. What happens? | They may take much longer to meet, or miss each other | They will meet faster because both of them are moving towards each other | Moving makes her easier to hear | Searchers always guess where she is walking
test: A lost girl is afraid that her mum will be angry. Searchers are calling her name. What is the right thing to do? | Answer them, because she is not in trouble | Stay quiet until they go away | Hide so they do not see her crying | Wait until her mum comes and calls her name herself
test: Which is true? | A child who stays in one place is usually found faster | A lost child should keep walking until they find a road | Searchers start looking far from where the child was last seen | Hiding helps searchers find you
test: A boy lost on a cold evening has a bright red jacket and a whistle. What should he do? | Put on the jacket, stay put and blow three blasts on the whistle | Take off the jacket so he can run faster | Hide the jacket so animals do not see it | Save the whistle for the morning, when there are more people about to hear it
test: A family in Sydney arrives at a big festival. What can they do first to be ready if a child gets lost? | Agree on a meeting point, like the entrance gate | Agree to shout as loud as possible | Agree that each child looks after themselves all day | Agree to search the car park first
test: A girl gets lost at a large Irish beach. Whom should she go to? | The lifeguards at their station | Someone sitting alone in a parked car | The edge of the water to look around | The road to walk home on her own
test: Why do searchers ask lost children to stay put rather than come to find them? | Searchers can cover an area properly if the child is not moving | Children walk too slowly to be useful | It is against the law to walk in a forest | Children always walk round in circles and end up back in the same place
test: lost in the bush, walk to find the way? | No, stay put, hug a tree | Yes, keep walking | Yes, run to find the car | Climb to the top of a hill
test: heard them calling my name but scared i'll get told off | Answer, you're not in trouble | Hide until they go | Stay quiet just in case, they might be cross | Run away from them
test: lost and the ground's freezing | Sit on branches or your bag | Lie on the bare ground | Take off your jacket | Keep walking all night to stay warm
test: gonna lose u at the show, what then | Go to our agreed meeting place | Go home by bus | Leave with a stranger | Wait in the car park till it's dark
test: lost in the woods and it's getting dark, keep walking? | Stay put, keep warm and make noise | Keep walking until you find a road | Run to get out faster | Climb a tall tree to sleep
test: lost and soaked from the rain, what first | Find shelter and put on dry or extra layers | Keep walking in wet clothes, they'll dry as you go | Lie down in the open | Take off your coat to dry
test: Ava says u should hide from searchers if ur in trouble. right? | No, answer and go towards them, they want to help | Yes, they might be angry | Yes, hide until morning | Yes, unless they call your name really nicely
test: did u hear a girl got lost in the woods and hid from the searchers cos she thought she'd be in trouble | Real lesson: a lost child is never in trouble, so answer searchers and never hide | Real lesson: searchers are angry with lost children and tell their parents off | It proves woods are only dangerous for girls | It is gossip, so hiding is fine
test: my mate says if ur lost in the forest just keep walking downhill and you'll always find a town | Not reliable; stay in one place, as someone who stays still is easier to find | He is right, downhill always leads to towns and roads in every forest in the world | He is right, if you walk all night | He is right, but only in Canada
test: Noah got lost on scout camp and they had to search for him, people are calling him Lost Boy, should i | No, the nickname is unkind; it could happen to anyone | Yes, because it is funny | Yes, so he is more careful | Yes, but only at camp
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the manner in which searchers conduct a search? | They commence at the place where the person was last seen and search outward | They commence at the furthest point of the area and search inward towards the centre | They search only along the roads | They search at random throughout the area
exam: Identify the purpose served by a lost child hugging a tree. | It assists the child to remain calm and in one place | It renders the child invisible to animals | It warms the tree for searchers | It indicates the direction of north to the searchers
exam: It being given that a lost child must sit upon cold ground, select the measure consistent with established guidance. | To sit upon branches or a bag so as to remain warmer | To lie directly upon the wet earth | To remove the coat so as to sit upon it | To dig a hole and sit within it
exam: Select the response that is consistent with established guidance concerning searchers calling a lost child's name. | The child should answer and never hide | The child should remain silent until certain of their identity | The child should run away from unknown voices | The child should hide until daylight
